| HUD object picking / selection behavior flip | `SwgCuiHud.cpp:198-224`, `:1432-1436` | Without `allowTargetAnything`, id-less non-sittable tangibles are rejected at `:218-220`; minted ids can pass that specific gate and then `setLookAtTarget(foundObject->getNetworkId())`. | RISK: broader targetability than today. | RISK: same. |
| Target status / MFD | `SwgCuiTargets.cpp:133-146`; `SwgCuiStatusGround.cpp:1598-1640`, click sets both targets at `:860-867` | Any valid id with a resolvable tangible can become target-pane state and can be clicked back into look-at/intended. | RISK: new UI state for `.ilf`. | RISK: same. |
| Examine / attributes | `CommandCppFuncs.cpp:333-338`; `SwgCuiHudAction.cpp:588-609`; `ObjectAttributeManager.cpp:387-408`, `:478-488`, `:1124-1165`; `CuiIconManager.cpp:840-842`, `:1260-1272` | Examine resolves id locally, but tooltips/attribute panes queue `getAttributesBatch` to server using id strings. | RISK: server attribute requests for reserved ids. | RISK: same. |
| Sit/follow/client command behavior | Sit fallback changes at `CommandCppFuncs.cpp:697-727`; follow requires creature/ship at `:886-940`; command checks at `ClientCommandChecks.cpp:243-338`, range at `:350-393` | A formerly invalid chair id would now be sent as chair id instead of actor id; follow/combat checks resolve local object then type-filter. | RISK for sit-on-chair protocol; mostly SAFE/no-op for non-creature follow/combat. | RISK same. |

**Overall Verdict**

Reserved-band minting is viable only with an editor/runtime guard that prevents reserved ids from being sent to the server for look-at, intended-target, command queue, radial menu, and attribute requests. The safer band is negative `int64`: it avoids snapshot/server positive ranges, is not considered a fake client id, is excluded from `.ws` allocation by construction, and does not collide with cluster masking because signed `m_value <= 0x3fffffffffffff` returns unchanged. A high-positive band is acceptable only in the narrow range `INT32_MAX+1` through `0x3fffffffffffff`; anything at/above `0x40000000000000` interacts with cluster-id stripping, and anything at/above `0x4000000000000000` collides with the existing fake-id band.
